<<~ ahu #/core-inversion>>

! Circles — Kowloon Reference Model

> Source: github.com/jzellis/kowloon (Josh Ellis / jzellis)
> Seeded: 2026-05-05 — authoritative design reference for CirclesDoc

From Kowloon's `CLAUDE.md` (the repo's own design declaration):

> "Kowloon has ''no follow/unfollow system''. Instead, users organize people into ''Circles'' (like contact lists). Adding someone to a circle IS the follow. There are no followers/following counts, no social graph edges."

From the README (unfederated activities table):

> "''Follow/Unfollow'' — Relationship visibility is private; never federate."
> "''Add/Remove'' — Circles are local 'playlist-like' constructs; never federate membership changes."

This is social graph inversion: on centralized platforms, following creates a public-facing graph edge the platform owns and exposes. On Kowloon, that action — putting someone in your Following circle — creates no public record. The graph is sharded: each user's graph lives exclusively on their home server, cannot be queried from outside, and is never transmitted. No remote server can reconstruct who follows whom.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/circle-data-model>>

!! Circle Data Model (Kowloon schema)

From `schema/Circle.js`:

```
id          — "circle:<mongoId>@<domain>"  (Kowloon ID format)
type        — "Circle" (user-created) | "System" (auto-managed)
actorId     — owning user's Kowloon ID; one owner per circle, home-server only
name        — display name
summary     — optional description
members     — embedded array of Member subdocuments (full actor snapshots, cached)
memberCount — integer
to          — addressing scope for the circle object itself
canReply    — who may reply to content addressed to this circle
canReact    — who may react
signature   — RSA signature of id|name|to|sortedMemberIds (tamper-evident)
```

Key: members are ''embedded snapshots'' (full actor profile + `lastFetchedAt`), not join table references. The system does not call out to remote servers to check circle membership at read time — it uses the cached local snapshot.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/circle-types>>

!! Circle Types

''System Circles'' (auto-created per user on registration):
* `Following` — people the user actively reads
* `All Following` — superset: followed servers + circles
* `Circles` — tracks circle memberships (members are circle objects, not users)
* `Blocked`
* `Muted`

''Group System Circles'' (auto-created per group):
* `Admins`, `Moderators`, `Members`, `Blocked`, `Pending`

''User Circles'': operator-created personal lists (e.g. "Close Friends", "Work").

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/addressing-hierarchy>>

!! Addressing Hierarchy

Every content object carries `to`, `canReply`, `canReact`. When set to a circle ID:

```
@public          — anyone, federated
@<domain>        — server members only (creator's home server)
<circleId>       — only members of that circle (must be owned by creator)
<userId>         — only that user (DM)
(blank)          — creator only
```

Enforced at handler level (`ActivityParser/handlers/Add/index.js`): rejects if `targetCircle.actorId !== activity.actorId`. A circle can only be targeted by its owner.

''Remote circles are never resolved.'' Feed fan-out only checks local circle membership. "Privacy: we can't verify membership in remote circles. Performance: avoid fetching remote circle data." — `docs/FEED_FANOUT.md`.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/lararium-mapping>>

!! Lararium Mapping

> ''Coherence note (2026-06-29).'' The refined identity model resolves a Lararium group
> as an ''edge-closure'' — the reciprocal-edge fan over signed, typed, `lar:`-named
> member edges, //evaluated// as a query — never a central `CirclesDoc` object a single
> node owns as a roster
> (<<~ loulou lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/group-as-closure>>). So the mapping rows
> below that read a circle as one owner-held doc carry ''retire-residue'' framing: keep
> them as the faithful Kowloon prior-art trace they document, but for //our// design read
> `CirclesDoc`/`actorId`-ownership as the closure each member's own edge composes, and
> read eviction as a kāpae on the membership stack
> (<<~ loulou lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/membership-sense>>). The Kowloon
> reference itself stands unchanged — Kowloon legitimately uses a central object.

| Kowloon | Lararium |
|---|---|
| `type: "System"` | `kind: "System"` — auto-seeded at node boot |
| `type: "Circle"` | `kind: "Circle"` — operator-created |
| `actorId` | `bag: @circles-<tag>` — the plane of the FACE that holds the circle, named off that PersonaGroup's own tag |
| `members[]` (embedded snapshots) | `memberDids` (TW5 list of DIDs); snapshots live in IdentitiesDoc |
| `to/canReply/canReact` on circle | `addressingScope` field on CircleTiddler |
| `to/canReply/canReact` on content | tiddler fields on content tiddlers (planned) |
| `signature` | `encryptedShareHint` + Keyhive capability chain (S7) |
| Never-federate membership | Membership stays in the face's own `@circles-<tag>`, never in registry tiddlers |
| System circles per user | Seeded by `seedCirclesDoc()` when a FACE is founded — one set per face, never one per machine |
| Nexus authorization rings | NOT a circle. A nexus tier names who may act at a Nexus, never who a person reads — it answers to `@nexus` (design-only today; no code stands it) |

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/system-circles>>

!! System Circles to Auto-Seed

At `seedCirclesDoc()` time — once per FACE, into that face's own `@circles-<tag>` — write these system circle tiddlers:

```
@circles/following       — people this face actively reads
@circles/all-following   — superset: followed nodes + circles
@circles/circles         — tracks circle memberships
@circles/blocked
@circles/muted
```

''The titles keep the bare namespace; the BAG carries the tag.'' A title resolves verbatim inside its own document, so `@circles/following` spells the same in every face's plane and one internal shape serves them all. Which face a follow belongs to reads off the BAG — `@circles-<tag>` — and a vessel-global `@circles` would seat one persona's blocked list in the same document as another's follows, where anything reading it correlates the faces a multitude exists to hold apart.

''Nexus authorization rings name no circle.'' A nexus tier says who may act at a Nexus, not who a person reads, so it answers to `@nexus` — one ring per Nexus rather than one per mask. Seeding `nexus:user` or `nexus:admin` into a face's circles plane would mint an authorization ring per face and file it under a follow graph. The rings stand as design today; no code seeds them.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-same-law-twice>>

!! ★ The non-federating relation ~ one law, found twice, at two layers ★

Kowloon's rule for the SOCIAL graph:

> //"Follow/Unfollow — Relationship visibility is private; never federate."// · //"Add/Remove — Circles are local 'playlist-like' constructs; never federate membership changes."// ''No remote server can reconstruct who follows whom.''

And `mesh/dyad.ts`'s rule for the DEVICE fleet, reached independently:

> ''the fleet EVALUATES AS A QUERY over that binding and never instantiates: nothing stores a fleet object to seize, forge, or sync''… //"a captured vessel spills the dyads admitted TO IT and never the human's map of themselves."//

''Two layers, one law: NO RELATION EVER BECOMES A SHARED OBJECT.'' No one else holds a circle as a graph edge; no one else holds a fleet as a roster. In both, ''a presented edge travels and the map stays home''.

!!! Why that ANSWERS the scaling question

The question //how far up does the inversion scale?// has a mechanical answer here rather than a hopeful one: ''it scales exactly as far as it needs nothing assembled to work''. A design scales while the relation stays a local query; it stops the moment something needs the whole graph — a roster, a follower count, a membership CRDT replicated to every member.

⚠ ''And that names the cost honestly.'' Anything wanting a global view of the relation — a recommendation feed, a mutual-friends count, a network-wide reputation — ''cannot build on this, and names no missing feature''. The trade gave it away. `dyad.ts` prices the same trade at the device layer: the membership-CRDT family //"buys convergence by replicating the op graph to everyone, handing each member the roster and the device-to-human linkage."//

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/not-adopted>>

!! What Lararium Does NOT Adopt from Kowloon

* MongoDB schema — we use Automerge CRDTs + tiddler fields
* ActivityPub fan-out — we use automerge-repo sync + oracle tiddlers
* RSA signature on circle state → replaced by Keyhive convergent capabilities (S7)
* `Members` as embedded subdocuments → member identity data lives in IdentitiesDoc; CirclesDoc holds only DIDs

<<~/ahu>>

